DECLARE `o 4 r 3(5) the relevant matter or proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `o 4 r 3(5)(a) a matter or proceeding commenced in, or an appeal under a written law from a tribunal to, the Court of Appeal`
    `o 4 r 3(5)(b) a matter or proceeding commenced in, or an appeal under a written law from a tribunal to, the Appellate Division`
    `o 4 r 3(5)(c) a matter or proceeding commenced in the General Division and any appeal from it`
    `o 4 r 3(5)(d) a matter or proceeding commenced in a Family Court and any appeal from it`
    `o 4 r 3(5)(e) a matter or proceeding commenced in a District Court and any appeal from it`
    `o 4 r 3(5)(f) a matter or proceeding commenced in a Magistrate's Court and any appeal from it`
    `o 4 r 3(5) none of these`

-- src:385-396 | (5) For the purposes of section 34(1)( ea ) and ( eb ) and (3) of the Legal Profession Act and in this Rule, “relevant matter or proceeding” means — (
-- src:385-396 | a ) any matter or proceeding commenced in, or any appeal under any written law from any tribunal to, the Court of Appeal; ( b ) any matter or
-- src:385-396 | proceeding commenced in, or any appeal under any written law from any tribunal to, the Appellate Division; ( c ) any matter or proceeding commenced in
-- src:385-396 | the General Division and any appeal from that matter or proceeding; ( d ) any matter or proceeding commenced in a Family Court and any appeal from
-- src:385-396 | that matter or proceeding; ( e ) any matter or proceeding commenced in a District Court and any appeal from that matter or proceeding; and ( f ) any
-- src:385-396 | matter or proceeding commenced in a Magistrate’s Court and any appeal from that matter or proceeding.
@ref O 4 r 3(5) (src:385-396)
GIVEN category IS A `o 4 r 3(5) the relevant matter or proceeding`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 4 r 3(5) the matter or proceeding is a relevant matter or proceeding` MEANS
    CONSIDER category
    WHEN `o 4 r 3(5) none of these` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“relevant matter or proceeding” means" the six heads (a) to (f), and none holds
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

